## Summary
Restore reporting of xfail markers added dynamically with `request.node.add_marker(...)` during a test. The call-phase report now refreshes the cached xfail evaluation so a failure is classified using the marker present when the test finishes, rather than only the markers present before it ran.

The change is confined to call-phase report generation; setup and teardown reports continue to use the existing stored evaluation. If reevaluation raises, the previous evaluation is retained.

## Regression coverage
Add an integration-test matrix that runs real nested pytest sessions and checks both outcome counts and exit status. It covers:
- the reported dynamically marked failing test;
- dynamically marked passing tests (XPASS), including strict XPASS;
- true and false marker conditions;
- matching and nonmatching `raises` restrictions;
- `--runxfail` retaining ordinary failure behavior;
- an unmarked failure as a control;
- an existing static xfail marker as a compatibility control.

The reported failing-test case distinguishes this fix from the original behavior: without call-phase reevaluation, that nested test is reported as failed instead of xfailed.

## Scope and trade-offs
This restores runtime marker handling without changing marker APIs or the runner lifecycle. Call-phase reevaluation can evaluate existing conditions again, and the fallback deliberately preserves the cached result rather than surfacing reevaluation errors. The tests focus on observable outcome semantics; they do not claim to eliminate those trade-offs.

## Summary
Fix `GaussianMixture.fit_predict(X)` returning labels from the last initialization rather than from the best initialization when `n_init > 1`.

The final E-step now runs after restoring `best_params`. Consequently, the returned labels and subsequent `predict(X)` calls use the same fitted parameters. The existing best-run selection, convergence handling, and fitted attributes are unchanged.

## Regression coverage
Add parameterized tests comparing `fit_predict(X)` with `predict(X)` on the same fitted estimator. Coverage includes:
- `n_init=1` and `n_init=5`;
- all four covariance types;
- random and k-means initialization;
- a single EM iteration and normal convergence;
- multiple fixed random seeds on reproducible overlapping data.

The single-iteration cases also protect the existing requirement that a final E-step is performed even when fitting stops early. Expected convergence warnings are suppressed locally; label mismatches remain failures.

Test command: `pytest sklearn/mixture/tests/test_fit_predict_restarts.py`

These tests are provided as regression coverage; no test execution is claimed here.

## Summary
Preserve input column dtypes when constructing pandas output from an array-backed transformer result. Output columns are matched to input dtypes by name, so feature selectors can retain metadata such as categorical ordering and narrow floating-point types even after selecting or reordering columns.

The implementation is confined to the pandas output wrapper. Default array output is unchanged, unmatched output columns keep their inferred dtype, and incompatible casts fall back to the inferred dtype. Already-DataFrame outputs retain their existing dtypes.

## Tests
Adds regression coverage for:
- SelectKBest preserving float16 and ordered categorical columns, category metadata, values, and a non-default index;
- default array output remaining unchanged;
- dtype lookup by column name rather than input position;
- dtype specifications supplied as either a Series or a dict;
- unmatched columns and incompatible casts;
- existing DataFrame output retaining its dtype;
- floating-point StandardScaler output retaining transformed values.

## Scope and limitations
This implements automatic dtype restoration under the existing pandas-output setting, not a separate dtype-preservation option. It does not determine whether an estimator changes values. Consequently, restoring an integer input dtype after a value-changing transformation can be lossy; successful casts are not necessarily lossless. A follow-up safety gate or explicit preservation policy is needed before treating this as a general solution for arbitrary transformers. The tests exercise the supported preservation paths without asserting that arbitrary dtype restoration is safe.
